<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Uqaylid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">990 CE – 1096 CE (approx.) · capital Mosul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Bedouin Arab dynasty that ruled Mosul and the Jazira for a century between the Buyids and the Seljuks, at times reaching Aleppo.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Banu Uqayl were an Arab tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Jazira</a> who served the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hamdanid-emirate-of-mosul">Hamdanid emirate of Mosul</a>. Around 990, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyid</a> power waned, the Uqaylid chief Muhammad ibn al-Musayyab took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, and his family went on to rule northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and parts of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Uqaylids ruled as tribal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, relying on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> cavalry and taxing the farmlands and trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Jazira</a>. They were Shia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qirwash-ibn-al-muqallad">Qirwash ibn al-Muqallad</a>, who reigned for about half a century from 1001, briefly recognised the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> in 1010 before returning to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-ibn-quraysh">Muslim ibn Quraysh</a>, called Sharaf al-Dawla, extended Uqaylid rule to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in 1080, making the dynasty a major power in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-ibn-quraysh">Muslim ibn Quraysh</a> was killed in 1085 fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-ibn-qutalmish">Sulayman ibn Qutalmish</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a>. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> in 1096, and Uqaylid branches lingered only in a few fortresses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qalat-jabar">Qal'at Ja'bar</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/uqaylid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Uqaylid dynasty" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Ali Zifan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uqaylid_dynasty_(greatest_extent).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>990 CE – 1096 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mosul</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad ibn al-Musayyab, Qirwash ibn al-Muqallad, Muslim ibn Quraysh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>, <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Uqaylids, Banu Uqayl</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1043&amp;focus=uqaylid-dynasty" aria-label="Uqaylid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5765 867 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Uqaylid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6085 1123L5936 1127M6085 1123L5936 1127M6085 1123L6481 1127"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5936 1127h0M5936 1127h0M6481 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6085 1123h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6085 1123h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mosul. <a href="/atlas?year=1043&amp;focus=uqaylid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
